To find the heights, first compute the angle $CAB$ using the law of cosines:

\begin{align*}
   \cos \angle CAB   &= \frac{5^2 + 5^2 - 6^2}{2 \cdot 5 \cdot 5} \\
   \angle CAB        &= \arccos\left(\frac{25 - 36}{50}\right) \\
   \angle CAB        &= 47.3608^\circ \\
\end{align*}

Now we can find the height from $B$, $BE$, using the Pythagorean theorem:

\begin{align*}
   BE^2   &= 5^2 - \frac{HE^2}{2} \\
   HE^2  &= 25 - BE^2 \\
   HE    &= \sqrt{25 - BE^2} \\
   HE    &= \sqrt{25 - (5^2 - \frac{HE^2}{2})} \\
   HE^3  &= 125 - 25HE - \left(\frac{HE^2}{2}\right)^2 \\
   HE    &= \sqrt[3]{125 - 25HE - \frac{HE^4}{8}}
\end{align*}

To find the quadratic equation for $x = HE^2$:

\begin{align*}
0 &= 125 - 25HE - \frac{HE^4}{8} \\
x^2 - 200x + 40^3 &= 0 \\
\end{align*}
The solutions for $x$ are $x = 40$ and $x = 160$. We know that $HE^2 \geq 0$, so $x = HE^2 = 40$.

Finally, use this value of $HE^2$ to compute the height $HE$:

\begin{align*}
   HE    &= \sqrt{40} \\
   HE    &= 6.3245 \ang{}^{\text{unitless}}
\end{align*}

The height from $A$, $AD$, is $6.3245/3 \approx 2.11$ unit(s).

Since $AB = BC$ and both points are the base of the right triangles $ADC$ and $DBE$, the triangle $DEC$ is equilateral and hence isosceles, so the two heights are equal. Thus, the area of triangle $DEC$ is $1/2 \cdot DE \cdot HE$:

\begin{align*}
   \text{Area of triangle }DEC &= \frac{1}{2} \cdot \frac{5}{2} \cdot (\sqrt{40}) \\
   \text{Area of triangle }DEC &= \boxed{7.50} \text{ square units }
\end{align*}

