We begin by calculating the height of the pyramid. In both equilateral triangle $PAD$ and regular hexagon base $ABCDEF$, the distance from the center to the side of the figure is $r$. Since $PAD$ and the triangle formed by $PAB, PBD,$ and $PED$ are equilateral triangles with equal heights, the height of the pyramid (side $PF$) is $2r$.

We then calculate the side length of the regular hexagon base. Since the sum of the angles of the hexagon is $720^{\circ}$, the vertex angle $\angle BCD$ is $\angle BCD = 120^{\circ}$. By the Law of Sines, we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{\text{side } BD}{\sin \angle BCD} &= \frac{r}{\sin 60^{\circ}} \\
\text{side } BD &= \frac{r \cdot \sin 60^{\circ}}{\sin 120^{\circ}}
\end{align*}

Since the base $ABCDEF$ is regular and center $O$ is equidistant from all six vertices, we have $r = \text{side } CDEF/2$. Substitute this into our expression for the side $BD$:
\begin{align*}
\text{side } BD &= \frac{\text{side } CDEF/2 \cdot \sin 60^{\circ}}{\sin 120^{\circ}} \\
&= \frac{\text{side } CDEF/2}{\tan 30^{\circ}} \\
&= \frac{\text{side } CDEF \cdot \sqrt{3}}{4}
\end{align*}

Now the edge $AB$ is half the apothem of the hexagon, so $\text{side } AB = \text{apothem } =\frac{\text{side } CDEF}{\sqrt{3}}$. With this, we can find the other edges of the pyramid and sum them up. ($\text{side } PB = \text{side } PD$)

First, side $PE$ and side $PH = \text{side } AB + \text{side } BD$:
\begin{align*}
\text{side } PE + \text{side } PH &= \text{side } AB + \text{side } BD \\
&= \frac{\text{side } CDEF}{\sqrt{3}} + \frac{\text{side } CDEF \cdot \sqrt{3}}{4} \\
&= \frac{4\text{side } CDEF + \text{side } CDEF\sqrt{3}}{4\sqrt{3}} \\
&= \frac{5\text{side } CDEF \sqrt{3}}{12}
\end{align*}

Now the side that forms two equilateral triangle sub-faces $PGH$ and $PEA$ has the height equal to the half of the height of pyramid (i.e., $r$) and base equal to $\text{side } PE$. By the area formula for equilateral triangles, we find their side length to be
\begin{align*}
\text{side } PG &= \sqrt{\frac{3}{4}}\text{side } PE \\
&= \frac{\sqrt{3}\cdot 5\text{side } CDEF}{12\sqrt{4}} \\
&= \frac{5\text{side } CDEF}{24}
\end{align*}
Side $PH$ equals $PG + PG = \frac{5\text{side } CDEF}{12}$.

Finally, side $PA = PD + DB$:
\begin{align*}
\text{side } PA &= PD + DB \\
&= 6 + \frac{\text{side } CDEF \cdot \sqrt{3}}{4} \\
&= \frac{24\text{side } CDEF \sqrt{3} + 6\text{side } CDEF}{24\sqrt{3}} \\
