\begin{questions}

\question

Let $m$ be a 3-digit positive integer such that $\mathop{\text{lcm}}[8m,10^{10}] = 4\cdot\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]$.\\
Find the value of $m$.

\begin{solution}

By definition of Least Common Multiple (LCM):

$$ 8m = \ell \cdot \mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}], \qquad
10^{10} = 4\cdot \ell' \cdot \mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}], \quad
\ell, \ell' \in \mathbb{N}. $$

From these equations,

$$ \frac{8m}{10^{10}} = \frac{\ell}{4\cdot \ell'} \quad \Rightarrow \quad m = \frac{\ell}{4} \times \frac{10^{10}}{4\ell'} \times 10^{-3}. $$

Since $m$ is a 3-digit number, we know that $\frac{10^{10}}{4\ell'}$ can only result in multiples of $60^3$ (since $10^{10}$ ends in five zeroes). Therefore, to find $m$ we can take either $\ell$ or $4\ell'$ to be fixed and large enough to satisfy this requirement (e.g., $\ell \geq 240,000$).\newline

However, the problem is not providing any specific conditions regarding the values of $\ell$ and $\ell'$, preventing us from solving the problem further.

\end{solution}

\end{questions}
\end{document}
```

