Let's first understand the given condition. The least common multiple (lcm) of two numbers $a$ and $b$ can be expressed by the product of the given numbers divided by their greatest common divisor (gcd). We can rewrite our condition:

\begin{align*}
\frac{\mathop{\text{lcm}}[8m,10^{10}]}{4} = \mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]
\end{align*}

Now notice that $\gcd(8m, 10^{10}) = \gcd(m, 10^{10})$, so we have a simple equation:

\begin{align*}
\frac{\mathop{\text{lcm}}[8m,10^{10}]}{4} = \frac{\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]}{1}
\end{align*}

From this, we can see that $\mathop{\text{lcm}}[8m,10^{10}]$ must be four times larger than $\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]$:

\begin{align*}
\mathop{\text{lcm}}[8m,10^{10}] = 4\cdot\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]
\end{align*}

Now, since $m$ is a 3-digit positive integer, and $\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]$ is a multiple of $10^{10}$, we can deduce that:

\begin{align*}
\mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}] = k \cdot 10^{10}, \quad k > 1
\end{align*}

As the $\mathop{\text{lcm}}$ of two numbers must be an integer, the only possibility for a 3-digit $m$ that satisfies the above condition is:

\begin{align*}
m &= \frac{\mathop{\text{lcm}}[8m,10^{10}]}{4\cdot 10^{10}} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{1}{4} \cdot (2^3 \cdot 10^3 \text{ or } 3^3 \cdot 10^3)}
\end{align*}

(Note: The two options for $m$ are $1625$ and $27000$, which correspond to 3-digit positive integers. Both are indeed factors of $4 \cdot \mathop{\text{lcm}}[m,10^{10}]$.)

