$$
\DeclareMathOperator\lcm{lcm}
$$
We will use the fundamental properties of $\text{lcm}$. Let's use the fact that for any positive integers $a, b$, we have:

1. $\lcm(a, b) = ab$ when $\text{gcd}(a, b) = 1$
2. $\lcm(a, b) = \frac{|ab|}{\text{gcd}(a, b)}$

We know $\lcm(8m, 10^{10}) = 4\lcm(m, 10^{10})$.

Assuming the $\text{gcd}(m, 10) = 1$, since $m$ is a 3-digit number, we use property 1:

\begin{align*}
\lcm(8m, 10^{10}) &= 8m\cdot \text{gcd}(8,10^{10})\\
\lcm(8m, 10^{10}) &= 8m\cdot\frac{8\cdot10^{10}}{\text{gcd}(8, 10)} &&\text{(Using property 2)}\\
\lcm(8m, 10^{10}) &= 4\lcm(m, 10^{10}) \\
8m\cdot \frac{8\cdot10^{10}}{\text{gcd}(8, 10)} &= 4\cdot\lcm(m, 10^{10}) \\
2m\cdot 10^{10} &= 4\cdot\lcm(m, 10^{10})
\end{align*}

Now divide both sides by $2\cdot 10^{10}$:

\begin{align*}
m &= 2 \cdot \frac{\lcm(m, 10^{10})}{10^{10}} \\
m &= \boxed{2 \cdot\underbrace{\frac \lcm\lbrace m, 10^{10}\rbrace} {10^{10}}}
\end{align*}

Since $m$ is a 3-digit positive integer, $\lbrace 10^2, 10^3 \rbrace \subseteq \lbrace \lcm(m, 10^{10}), 2 \cdot \lcm(m, 10^{10})\rbrace$. However, neither $10^{2}$, nor $10^{3}$ are 3 times a 3-digit number. Therefore, $m$ must be one of $10^{2}$ or $10^{3}$. However, for $m=10^{2}$ and $m=10^{3}$, the left-hand side is not equal to the right-hand side. Hence, our assumption that $\text{gcd}(m,10)=1$ is false.

Therefore, we have a contradiction. No such $m$ meets the given conditions.

\boxed{No solution exists}

