Here is a solution to the problem in LaTeX:

The first 20 positive integers and their product are:

\begin{equation*}
  1, \, 2, \, 3, \, .\, . \, . \, , \, 20 \quad \text{and} \quad 1 \times 2 \times 3 \times . \, . \, . \, \times 20 = 2,432,902,008
\end{equation*}

To find a positive integer that is greater than 1 and relatively prime to 2,432,902,008, it is useful to factorize 2,432,902,008.

\begin{equation*}
  2,432,902,008 = 2^5 \times 3 \times 11 \times 23 \times 29 \times 73 \times 97 \times 193 \times 383
\end{equation*}

We can begin by listing the prime factors of the given number and cross them out as we go along:

\begin{enumerate}
   \item Not possible to subtract a multiple greater than 1 of 2 (since it is a factor of the product)
   \item $2^1$: Subtract $2$ from the product

        \begin{equation*}
          2,432,902,008 - 2 \times 3 \times 11 \times 23 \times 29 \times 73 \times 97 \times 193 \times 383 = 2,432,901,990
        \end{equation*}

   \item $3$: Subtract a multiple of 3

         \begin{equation*}
          2,432,901,990 - 3 \times 11 \times 23 \times 29 \times 73 \times 97 \times 193 \times 383 = 2,432,898,287
         \end{equation*}

   \item Not possible to subtract a multiple greater than 1 of 11 and 23 at the same time (since $\gcd(11, 23) = 1$)

         If we let $m = \gcd(11, 23)$, then by the Euclidean algorithm:

        \begin{align*}
          23 & = 2 \times 11 + 1 \\
          11 & = 10 + 1 \\
          10 & = 10
        \end{align*}

        Thus, $m = 1$.

        It is not possible to subtract multiples of prime numbers $11$ and $23$ since both are relatively prime.

   \item Not possible to subtract a multiple greater than 1 of 29 and 73 at the same time (since $\gcd(29, 73) = 1$)

         If we let $m = \gcd(29, 73)$, then by the Euclidean algorithm:

        \begin{align*}
           73 & = 2 \times 29 + 17 \\
           29 & = 17
        \end{align*}

        Thus, $m = 1$.

        It is not possible to subtract multiples of prime numbers $29$ and $73$ since both are relatively prime.

   \item Not possible to subtract a multiple greater than 1 of 97 and 193 at the same time (since $\gcd(97, 193) = 1$)

         If we let $m = \gcd(97, 193)$, then by the Euclidean algorithm:

        \begin{align*}
          193 & = 2 \times 97 + 11 \\
          97 & = 97
        \end{align*}

        Thus, $m = 1$.

        It is not possible to subtract multiples of prime numbers $97$ and $193$ since both are relatively prime.

   \item $383$: Subtract a multiple of $383$.

         \begin{equation*}
          2,432,898,287 - 383 \times 97 \times 193 \times 383 = 2,432,896,000
         \end{equation*}

   \item Not possible to subtract a multiple greater than 1 of 2, as we already subtracted its power of 5.
\end{enumerate}

As a result, no smaller positive integers exist that are relatively prime to the product of the first 20 positive integers. The smallest such number is 2,432,896,001.

\boxed{2,432,896,001 \text{ units } }

