\begin{document}
Since no member can hold more than one office, the number of ways to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer is equal to the number of ways to choose 3 members out of 20.

The number of ways to choose 3 members out of 20 is given by the binomial coefficient:

\boxed{ \binom{20}{3} }

To calculate this, we use the formula:

\binom{n}{k} = \frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}

Substitute:

n = 20
k = 3

\boxed{ \frac{ (20)! }{ (3)!( (20)-(3) )! } }

Now calculate the factorials:

20! = 20 \cdot 19 \cdot 18 \cdot \ldots \cdot 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1
3! = 3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1
17! \approx 1.40801 \times 10^{17} % we ignore this because the result will be so large

\boxed{  \frac{ (20 \cdot 19 \cdot 18) }{ (3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1) } }

Factor out the number 2:

\boxed{ \frac{ (10 \cdot 9 \cdot 18) }{ 6 } }

Now calculate the desired number:

\boxed{ \frac{ (10 \cdot 9 \cdot 18) }{ 6 } }

\boxed{ 30 \times 3 }
\end{document}
