Hint: Recall the graph of an even function looks the same when reflected across the y-axis.

Step 1: What is the definition of an even function?
An even function satisfies the property $f(x) = f(-x)$.

Step 2: For an even function $f(x)$, we have the following:
\begin{align*}
f(x) & = f(-x)  \\
-f(-x) & = -f(x)
\end{align*}

Step 3: To evaluate $f(0)$, simply evaluate $f(-0) = f(0)$ since $f$ is even.  Use the given functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$.

Step 4: Let $f(x) = x^2$ and $g(x)= x^3$. Show that their product $f(x) g(x) = x^5$ is even by evaluating at $x=0$ and $x=0=-0$, which will yield the same value.
\begin{align*}
f(0) g(0) & = x^2 \cdot x^3 \Bigg|_{x=0}  \\
& = 0^2 \cdot 0^3 \\
& = 0.
\end{align*}

Step 5: Let's find the graph of $f(x)g(x)$ over various domains, say from $[-4,4]$ using the unit step function $\mathbbm{1}(x)$ to show it's symmetric about the y-axis.
\begin{align*}
\mathbbm{1}(x) \cdot f(x) g(x) & = \mathbbm{1}(x) \cdot x^5  \\
& = \begin{cases}
5x^4 & \text{ if } x > 0 \\
5 (-x)^4 & \text{ if } x < 0 \qquad \text{( since $f$ and $g$ are even)}
\end{cases} \\
& = 5 (-1)^4 x^4 & \text{ if } x < 0 \\
& = 5x^4 & \text{ if } x > 0.
\end{align*}

Step 6: Plot the graph of $\mathbbm{1}(x) \cdot f(x) g(x)$ in the interval $[-4,4]$ for visual confirmation:
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[
