Using the given equations, we can manipulate the expression as follows:
\begin{align*}
\cos^3 x + \cos^2 x
&=\cos^2 x (\cos x + 1) \\
&= (\sin^2 x + 1)^2 - \sin^2 x \\
&= \sin^4 x + 2\sin^2 x + 1. \boxed{2\sin^2 x + 1}
\end{align*}
Since the numbers $\sin x,$ $\cos x,$ $\tan x$ form a geometric sequence, it implies that $\sin^2 x = \dfrac{1}{1 + \cos^2 x}$.
Now we substitute this result into the expression:
\begin{align*}
\boxed{2\sin^2 x + 1}
&=\boxed{2\left(\dfrac{1}{1 + \cos^2 x}\right)^2 + 1} \\
&=\boxed{\dfrac{2}{(1 + \cos^2 x)^2} + 1} \boxed{<solution>}
\end{align*}

