Let's solve for the roots of $Q_1(x)$ and $Q_2(x)$. Then we will use these roots to express the discriminant of $P(x)$ in terms of the polynomial coefficients, reducing it to a quadratic equation in $k$. Solving this quadratic equation will give us the desired maximum value of $k$, as desired.

First, we find the roots of $Q_1(x)$ by solving the quadratic equation:

\begin{align*}
x^2 + (k-29)x -k &= 0 \\
x &= \frac{-k+29 \pm \sqrt{(k-29)^2 +4k}}{2}
\end{align*}

Next, we find the roots of $Q_2(x)$, which can be done in the same manner:

\begin{align*}
2x^2 + (2k-43)x + k &= 0 \\
x &= \frac{-(2k-43) \pm \sqrt{(2k-43)^2 -4\cdot2\cdot k}}{4}
