As the pentagon and the hexagon share a common side with vertices A, D, and C, side AD is the diple of the apothem of the regular polygons. Let $r$ be the apothem.
Since the apothem of a regular pentagon divided by the apothem of a regular hexagon is $\frac{4}{\phi}$, we have the following relationship:

\begin{align*}
\frac{AD}{AP} &= \frac{4}{\phi} & \text{(apothem of regular pentagon with respect to apothem of regular hexagon)} \\
\frac{AD}{AE} &= \frac{4}{\phi} \cdot \frac{\phi - 1}{2} && \text{(apothem of a regular polygon in terms of the apothem and side length)} \\
AD &= \frac{8}{\phi - 1}
\end{align*}

The side lengths of the regular hexagon can be found by dividing the circumradius (which is equal to $2r$) by the cosine of 60 degrees, which is $\frac{1}{2}$, as shown below:

\begin{align*}
AE &= 2r \cdot \cos(60) \\
&= \frac{r}{2}\\
AD &= 2 \quad \text{(since they share a common side)}
\end{asy}


