Solution:Let the number of quarters be Q. When Q is divided by 5, the number left over is 3:
\begin{equation*}
Q = 5 \times (\text{number of piles}) + 3
\end{equation*}
When Q is divided by 7, the number left over is 5:
\begin{equation*}
Q = 7 \times (\text{new number of piles}) + 5
\end{equation*}
Let's solve a system of equations:
\begin{align*}
5 \times (\text{number of piles}) + 3 &= Q\\
7 \times (\text{new number of piles}) + 5 &= Q
\end{align*}
First, isolate Q:
\begin{align*}
Q - 3 &= 5 \times (\text{number of piles})\\
Q - 5 &= 7 \times (\text{new number of piles})
\end{align*}
Next, subtract the first equation from the second:
\begin{align*}
-(2 \times \text{number of piles}) + 2 &= -2 \times (\text{new number of piles})\\
2 &= (\text{new number of piles}) - 2 \times (\text{number of piles})
\end{align*}
Re-writing as a fraction, we have:
\begin{equation*}
\frac{2}{2 \times (\text{number of piles}) - 1} = \text{new number of piles}
\end{equation*}
Now we can take advantage of the fact that Wendy has less than ten dollars worth of quarters. The maximum value of Q is $9\times25 = 225$. We also obtain from the problem that the number of piles must be at least 5 and at most 3+:
\begin{align*}
(\text{number of piles}) \in [5, 7] \implies 5 \leq \frac{225}{2x-1} \leq 7
\end{align*}
For $x = 2.5, 3$, we have:
\begin{align*}
45 \leq \frac{225}{2\times3-1} \leq 60
\end{align*}
Thus, it may be possible for Wendy to have 45, 60, or 75 quarters, but we will eliminate the impossible cases by considering whether 28, 42, or 60 fits the picture of Wendy having less than ten dollars worth of quarters. We only need to check the expression's numerator:
\begin{align*}
45 &\times 25 = 1125 < 10 \times 100\\
60 &\times 25 = 1500 \geq 10 \times 100
\end{align*}
Hence, Wendy must have exactly 45 quarters.
\begin{equation*}
\boxed{\text{exact_value} = 45 \quad \text{quarters}}

