We know that $\cos$ is periodic and its period is $360^\circ$. So we want to find $n$ such that:
\begin{align*}
\cos n^\circ &= \cos (n + 360)^\circ = \cos (568 + 360)^\circ
\end{align*}
When we add $360^\circ$, the angle $568^\circ$ moves to the range $[0^\circ, 360^\circ]$.
\begin{align*}
\cos n^\circ &= \cos (568 + 360)^\circ &= \cos (928)^\circ \\
\text{Let } k &= \frac{928}{360} = 2 + \frac{1}{2} = 2 \tfrac{1}{2} \implies n^\circ &= 360k = 360 \cdot 2\tfrac{1}{2} = 180 + 180 \cdot \tfrac{1}{2} = 180 + 90 = 270^\circ \\
\implies n &= 270^\circ \cdot \frac{360}{360} = \boxed{270^\circ}
\end{align*}
This numerical value corresponds to the hour hand position on a clock looking south in the late evening. It is important to put the answer in terms of degrees for this question, as it emphasizes and follows the context presented, without the need for further clarification.

The hours on a clock are usually numbered from 1 to 12, so to write this answer in terms of hours, we need to divide by 30:
\begin{align*}
\boxed{\frac{270}{30}} \approx 9\text{ hours}
\end{align*}

