First, we simplify each side of the equation by finding the least common multiple for the denominators of trigonometric functions:

On the left-hand side, the function is sine with a period of $2\pi$, and the angle is multiplied by 3. The least common multiple (LCM) of 1 and 3 is 3, so we can rewrite the angle as $\theta$ mod $2\pi/3$.

On the right-hand side, the function is cosine with a period of $2\pi$, and the angle is multiplied by 7. The LCM of 1 and 7 is 7, so we can rewrite the angle as $\theta$ mod $2\pi/7$.

Now we have:

\begin{equation}
\sin 3\theta = \cos 7\theta \quad\text{ with }\quad \theta \equiv \theta \mod \frac{2\pi}{3} \text{ and } \theta \equiv \theta \mod \frac{2\pi}{7}.
\end{equation}

