The volume of a cube is given by the formula $V = s^3$, where $s$ is the length of one side (edge) of the cube. The volume of the original cube, then, is $(2 \,\, \text{cm})^3 = 8 \,\, \text{cubic centimeters {[}V_{orig}{]}}$. The volume of the new cube is $8 \,\, \text{cubic centimeters {[}V_{orig}{]}} + 19 \,\, \text{cubic centimeters {[}V_{new}{]}$. Since the new cube is a cube as well, we can write the equation for its volume as $V_{new} = s_{new}^3$. Since the volume of both cubes is the same, we can set them equal to each other.

\[
\boxed{
V_{new} = 8 \,\,\text{cubic centimeters} + 19 \,\,\text{cubic centimeters} \\
s_{new}^3 = (8 + 19) \,\,\text{cubic centimeters} \\
s_{new} = \sqrt[3]{(8 + 19) \,\,\text{cubic centimeters}} \\
s_{new} = \sqrt[3]{27 \,\,\, \text{cubic centimeters}}
\]

\boxed{$s_{new} = 3 \sqrt[3]{3} \,\, \text{centimeters}$}

